    "math_academy_id": "q-74653",
    "knowledge_point_id": "730fc9a7-a1b8-55a4-b8d3-666f87b82958",
    "knowledge_point": "Dividing a Complex Number by an Imaginary Number",
    "problem": "If ${z}_{1}=5-2\\text{i}$ and ${z}_{2}=3\\text{i}$, then ${z}_{1}$ divided by ${z}_{2}$ equals",
    "worked_solution": "First, we set up the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5-2\\text{i}}{3\\text{i}}\n$$\n\nThe denominator is the imaginary number $3\\text{i}.$ Note that this can be written as $0+3\\text{i},$ so the conjugate is $0-3\\text{i},$ or simply $-3\\text{i}.$\n\nSo, we multiply both the numerator and the denominator by $-3\\text{i},$ so that the denominator turns into a real number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{z}_{1}}{{z}_{2}} & =\\frac{5-2\\text{i}}{3\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5-2\\text{i}}{3\\text{i}}\\cdot \\frac{-3\\text{i}}{-3\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-15\\text{i}+6{\\text{i}}^{2}}{-9{\\text{i}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-15\\text{i}+6(-1)}{-9(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-6-15\\text{i}}{9}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the denominator is a real number, we can write the complex number in the form $a+b\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-6-15\\text{i}}{9}=-\\frac{6}{9}-\\frac{15}{9}\\text{i}=-\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{5}{3}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
